# b14 — async sequences: a list's method that an async sequence still does
# not have, `uniq`, called after a chain (`map`). The refusal names the
# chain's type as an async sequence of what `map` gives, and its help lists
# every method it does have.
# Run with `lume check corpus/m116/b14_async_no_uniq.lume`.
# Meant to FAIL.
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
# error: an `async Iterator[Str]` has no `uniq` yet
#   --> corpus/m116/b14_async_no_uniq.lume:21:33
#    |
# 21 |   puts nums().map { |n| n.to_s }.uniq
#    |                                 ^
#   help: it has the chains `map`, `filter`, `reject`, `take_while`, `take`, `skip` and `enumerate`; `first`, `count`, `sum`, `any?`, `all?`, `find`, `each` and `fold`; and, keeping every item first, `join`, `max`, `min`, `max_by`, `min_by`, `sort`, `sort_by`, `last` and `reverse`; for the rest, keep its items with `.to_list`
# EXPECTED EXIT: 1

async def nums -> Iterator[Int]:
  yield 2
  yield 2

async def main:
  puts nums().map { |n| n.to_s }.uniq
